## {#units-of-derivative data-menu-title="Units of the Derivative"}

[Units of the derivative]{.slide-title}

<hr>

If we have a function $f(x)$, then 

:::{.center-text .teal-text}
the units for $f'(x)$ are the units of $f(x)$ divided by the units of $x$.
:::

<br>

$f'(x)$ is approximately equal to the change in $f(x)$ when the variable $x$ increases by 1 unit.

<br>

:::{style="color:green;"}
✏️ **Example:**
:::

- $g(t)=$ temperature in degrees Celsius at $t$ hours after midnight
- $g(7)=15$ means the temperature is 15°C at 7 hours after midnight (7 am)
- $g'(7)=3$ means the temperature is increasing at a rate of 3°C/ hour at 7am
- $g'(7)=-2$ means the temperature is decreasing at a rate of 2°C/hour at 7am

