## {#application-solution data-menu-title="Logistic growth" .smaller}

[Application: Logistic growth]{.slide-title}

<hr>

:::: {.columns}

::: {.column width="50%"}

(1) $\frac{dN}{dt}>0$ means the population is growing; $\frac{dN}{dt}<0$ means the population is shrinking; $\frac{dN}{dt}=0$ means the population size is momentarily unchanged.

<br>

(2) $\frac{dN}{dt}$ is **positive** for all $0<N<K$, since the population is always increasing toward the carrying capacity. $\frac{dN}{dt}$ is **zero** at $N=0$ and $N=K$, the two points where the population stops changing.

<br>

(3) Population growth is **fastest** at $N=K/2$, the inflection point of the curve, where the slope of $N(t)$ is steepest.

:::

